{"objective":"Sequence walkie's development so that technical risk is retired before it can invalidate committed work, and so that every phase ends with something the team can actually use rather than a half-finished layer.\n\nOnly phase 1 is decomposed into work items in this planning session. Later phases remain prose deliberately: the vision calls for growth driven by team feedback, and work items written for features the team has not yet used would claim a certainty that does not exist.","scope":"## Phase 0 — audio toolchain spike (blocking)\n\nProve on **real ARM hardware**, not on a development laptop, that miniaudio capture and libopus encoding build and run under the `voice` build tag, that zig cc cross-compilation produces working artifacts, and what the actual encode cost per frame is.\n\n*Why first:* it is the only item in the MVP whose failure would change the MVP's contents. The audio constraints finding establishes that no pure-Go encoder exists, so this dependency is unavoidable and must be tested rather than assumed.\n\n**Gate, with its consequence defined in advance:** if the spike fails, the voice-note work item is removed from phase 1 and the MVP ships as text and presence only. Phase 2 then absorbs all audio work.\n\n## Phase 1 — MVP: text, presence, voice notes\n\nThe full contents of scp:mvp-text-voicenote, decomposed into work items under execution container ctr:wave-1.\n\n*Why this shape:* it delivers four of the five use cases from the vision — coordination, emergency communication, file sharing by voice note and message, and the substrate for troubleshooting — without touching either of the two genuinely hard problems.\n\n*Internal ordering:* the protobuf schema and the coordinator skeleton come before the features that ride on them; reconnect and the offline queue come before voice notes, because a voice note is an attachment on a delivery path that must already work.\n\n## Phase 2 — real-time voice and the direct data plane\n\nReal-time one-to-one calls over UDP with RTP framing; the direct peer-to-peer data plane; file transfer with resume; the relay-fallback implementation from adr:001; and the direct-versus-relay and measured-latency indicators in the interface.\n\n*Why together:* these are one architectural step, not four features. The direct data plane is the prerequisite for all of them, and the relay fallback is meaningless until a direct path exists to fall back from. Latency validation belongs here because this is the first phase in which the sub-100 ms target applies at all — and it must be **measured on the team's own devices** before the requirement is treated as met.\n\n## Phase 3 — rooms and observation\n\nGroup chat with room persistence, invitation and removal. Read-only PTY streaming for collaborative debugging, with the sharer always able to see that sharing is active and to stop it instantly.\n\n*Why after phase 2:* rooms multiply the delivery paths that must already be reliable, and group voice — if it is ever wanted — depends on the direct data plane existing first. Group voice must not be built as a full mesh.\n\n## Phase 4 — extensibility\n\nSlash commands and aliases; webhooks outward on events; the subprocess plugin protocol; the auto-update mechanism with signed, checksum-verified releases.\n\n*Why last, and in this order:* the local control socket delivered in phase 1 already makes walkie scriptable, which covers most of the practical demand for automation. A plugin runtime is the largest of these items and the least validated by real use, so it goes behind everything the team has actually asked for twice.\n\n## Never\n\nRemote command execution. Declined by adr:005-no-remote-command-execution and delegated to Tailscale SSH. This is not a phase-5 item; it is out of the product.","outOfScope":"**Not sequenced by this roadmap.** Rich text formatting, emoji handling and attachment preview beyond voice notes; key rotation and multi-device identity; encrypted local message history. These are recorded gaps. The first two are named explicitly in adr:004-security-model as MVP limitations, and none of them has enough shape yet to be placed in a phase honestly.\n\n**Not decomposed by this roadmap.** Phases 2, 3 and 4 exist here as sequenced intent only. No work items, containers or tickets are created for them in this planning session. They are decomposed when the phase before them has shipped and the team has used it — which is what makes the ordering above a plan rather than a forecast.\n\n**Out of the product.** Remote command execution, per adr:005-no-remote-command-execution."}